statement:
For a positive integer $N$, let $T(N)$ denote the number of arrangements of the integers $1, 2, \ldots, N$ into a sequence $a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_N$ such that $a_i > a_{2i}$ for all $i$ with $1 \leq i < 2i \leq N$, and $a_i > a_{2i+1}$ for all $i$ with $1 \leq i < 2i+1 \leq N$. (These are the so-called heap sequences.)
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find $T(7)$.
\item[(b)] If $K$ is the largest non-negative integer so that $2^K$ divides $T(2^n - 1)$, show that $K = 2^n - n - 1$.
\item[(c)] Find the largest non-negative integer $K$ so that $2^K$ divides $T(2^n + 1)$.
\end{enumerate}
